desktop-shell-window-title-format(1)

# NAME

desktop-shell-window-title-format - adds the process ID to window title bars

# SYNOPSIS

*desktop-shell-window-title-format* [*-c* _FILE_]

# DESCRIPTION

*desktop-shell-window-title-format* sets the title bar text of every window,
by default to its title followed by its process ID in grey. It follows the
compositor through its IPC socket, found in _$SWAYSOCK_, sends a command only
when a window's text changes, and exits when the compositor does.

The default format uses Pango markup, which sway only renders when its font
is set with a *pango:* prefix. Title bars show only with *default_border
normal*, or in tabbed and stacked layouts.

# OPTIONS

*-h, --help*
	Show the help message and exit.

*-c, --config* _FILE_
	Read settings from _FILE_ instead of the usual places.

*-s, --style* _FILE_
	Accepted like by every component; unused, as the compositor draws the titles.

# CONFIGURATION

The installed _@sysconfdir@/sway/config.d/60-desktop-shell-window-title-format.conf_
starts the program with sway. A second copy started by a configuration reload
exits at once.

# SETTINGS

Read from _$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/desktop-shell-components/window-title-format.ini_,
else _/etc/xdg/desktop-shell-components/window-title-format.ini_, which lists
every setting with its default. All are optional; *swaymsg reload* applies
changes.

*[titles]*
	*enabled* = _true_|_false_ (false restores the plain titles)++
	*format* = text with sway's *%title* *%app_id* *%class* *%instance* *%shell*,
	plus *{pid}* *{name}* *{workspace}*; *{title}* means *%title*

The format may contain *"* or *'*, but not both, and must not end in a
backslash; otherwise the default is used.

# SEE ALSO

*sway*(5), *sway-ipc*(7), *desktop-shell-workspace-window-bar*(1)
